{{Politics of Balisca}}

In [[Balisca]], the '''Volisan aristocracy''' ({{lang-bl|Aristocracia volisano}}) was a social group that made up the [[W:social elite|social]] and {{W|political elite}} of the country once it gained independence in the early 19th century. The so-called aristocracy was formed by the descendants of the first [[Colonization of Balisca|colonizers of Balisca]] and by the Volisans who came to settle in the country in great numbers, in whom the [[Crown of Volisania]] entrusted the exercise of power in its overseas provinces. By the beginning of the 18th century this group had established itself as a landed and commercial aristocracy.

In the 16th century, a large part of the Illypnians that came to Balisca to settle or to conduct trade were [[W:Catalan language|Volisanian]] and [[W:Occitan language|Beron]]-speaking [[Catharism|Cathars]]. Thanks to their austere character and the commercial links they had with the [[Mesogean world]], many of these immigrants amassed considerable fortunes, and established matrimonial ties between themselves and the local elite, owners of large extensions of land and holders of social prestige.

This group quickly came to dominant Baliscan politics in the second half of the 18th century, retaining its position throughout the 19th century and the first half of the 20th century. As a way of consolidating their power, the purchase of titles of nobility or orders of chivalry became increasingly common in this group, as well as the establishment of ''{{W|Majorat|mayorazgos}}'', which allowed them to keep large properties without the need to divide them. The social standing of the Catalan-Basque aristocracy was left largely unharmed during the [[Volisanya Boba]] period and through the [[Baliscan Civil War]]. However, with the arrival of the [[Cantonal Revolution]], this influence was greatly reduced, and was swept away entirely amidst the upheaval of the [[Baliscan Revolution]].

People of Volisan ancestry have however continued to be over-represented in the [[Confederal Congress]] of Balisca since the Baliscan Revolution, albeit said overrepresentation is no longer as marked as it used to be in the 19th century.

